+Why the first version trained badly: the original ``swing_foot_clearance``
+charged the saturating shortfall through the ENTIRE airborne time below the
+threshold. A swing foot is a parabola -- even a perfect swing whose apex
+exactly meets the target spends most of the arc below it, so an ideal gait
+still ate ~60% of the max penalty. That permanent floor locked the policy in a
+tug-of-war against the smoothness terms (action rate / dof acc), which showed
+up in the logs as 3x jerk, 6x below-height terminations and a clearance penalty
+stuck at 47% of max.
+
+The redesign is the phase-free analogue of the cited paper's actual form
+(arXiv:2403.10723, Table II: ``-0.15 I_swing w(s) (1 - exp{-20 c_foot})`` with
+``w(s) = (1 + sin(pi s)) / 2`` -- ZERO at lift-off/touchdown, peaking mid-swing;
+the first version had dropped that weight entirely):
+
+  * mid-swing weighting without a clock -- during swing the foot's horizontal
+    speed follows a half-sine (zero at lift-off/touchdown, max mid-swing), so
+    ``w = clamp(|v_foot_xy| / v_ref, 0, 1)`` reproduces the paper's w(s) shape.
+    It is also exactly the failure mode that matters on hardware: a LOW foot
+    that is TRANSLATING is the one that scuffs;
+  * speed-scaled target -- ``h_target = h_low + (h_high - h_low) *
+    clip(|v_cmd| / speed_ref, 0, 1)``: creeping commands get a small target, so
+    the robot is never forced into high-step marching at near-zero speed;
+  * canonical gate -- active only when ``|v_cmd_xy| > 0.1`` like the official
+    ``feet_air_time`` (the old gate at the 0.025 deadband taxed the crawl band
